# Frontend Rules

## Stack

Use:

- Next.js
- React
- TypeScript
- React Query
- Axios
- React Hook Form
- Zod
- shadcn/ui
- next-intl

Do not introduce another library that duplicates an existing responsibility.

## Architecture

Use feature-based architecture.

Example:

features/conversions/
├── components/
├── hooks/
├── api/
├── schemas/
├── types/
└── index.ts

Keep feature-specific UI inside the feature.

Shared shadcn/ui primitives currently live in `apps/web/components/ui`.
Do not create `packages/ui` unless there is a clear cross-app reuse need.

## Server State

Use React Query for server state.

Do not manually synchronize server state with local React state when React Query is appropriate.

Use query keys consistently.

Invalidate or update relevant queries after mutations.

Do not duplicate API state inside Zustand or arbitrary global state.

## HTTP

Use the shared Axios API client.

Preferred:

Component
↓
Feature hook
↓
React Query
↓
API client
↓
Axios
↓
Hono API

Do not make raw fetch/Axios requests directly inside UI components.

## Forms

Use React Hook Form for non-trivial forms.

Use Zod for validation.

Keep schemas close to the owning feature unless they are genuinely shared.

Prefer:

useForm +
zodResolver +
Zod schema

Do not duplicate validation rules between form components.

## Components

Use shadcn/ui primitives.

Shared UI primitives currently live in `apps/web/components/ui`.

Feature-specific components remain inside their feature.

Do not create huge components.

Prefer composition.

If a component becomes difficult to understand, split it by responsibility.

## Server vs Client Components

Prefer Server Components by default.

Use Client Components only when client-side interactivity or browser APIs are required.

Do not mark entire routes as client components unnecessarily.

Keep data fetching and sensitive operations on the server where appropriate.

## Loading and Error States

Every asynchronous feature should consider:

- Loading state
- Empty state
- Error state
- Success state

Do not leave users with blank screens while requests are pending.

## Internationalization

Use next-intl.

All user-facing strings must be translatable.

Do not hardcode user-facing strings unnecessarily.

Keep translation keys organized by feature.

## Accessibility

Use semantic HTML.

Use accessible labels.

Ensure keyboard interaction works.

Do not rely only on color to communicate state.

Use shadcn/ui accessibility patterns where applicable.

## Styling

Use the existing design system and Tailwind conventions.

Do not introduce another styling solution without a strong reason.

Avoid arbitrary one-off styles when an existing design token or component can be used.

## Performance

Avoid unnecessary client components.

Avoid unnecessary re-renders.

Use React Query caching appropriately.

Do not download large files through the application server when direct S3 uploads/downloads are possible.

For file uploads:

Browser
↓
Presigned S3 URL
↓
S3

## Security

Never expose:

- API secrets
- S3 credentials
- Better Auth secrets
- Polar credentials

The frontend must never be the source of truth for authorization or plan enforcement.
